(259754, 17259, 4207, '<td valign="top">Ah man, i wish the resolution was higher. Good none the less.</td>', 'Ah man, i wish the resolution was higher. Good none the less.', '2003-04-08 23:18:25.000000', NULL, 1),
(259755, 17126, 5120, '<td valign="top">I like the subject of your picture but I think there are a lot of things that you could do to strengthen your photograph.  The first thing that comes to mind is that the colors look very washed out, almost as if the picture was overexposed by a stop or so.  The second thing that comes to mind is that there are a lot of distracting objects in this photo.  To list a few, the building on the left, the phone pole in the background, the driveway(?) on the right side and the yellow object that appears to be next to the driveway.  A lot of these things can be eliminated by using a wider aperture to throw the background out of focus.  I would increase the contrast and saturation also.  It might also help to just walk around the tree and take pictures from all different angles and distances and see what works the best.  It took me 4 days of trying to get pictures of my peach trees to get some shots that I liked.  What worked for me was a low angle and close to the tree.  Every tree is different and it will probably take time to find the best angle and lighting situation.  Usually early morning and late afternoon give the best light.  I hope some of this is helpful to you.  I gave this one a 4 but I think you could produce much better with a little more work.<br/> <br/>Greg<br/></td>', 'I like the subject of your picture but I think there are a lot of things that you could do to strengthen your photograph.  The first thing that comes to mind is that the colors look very washed out, almost as if the picture was overexposed by a stop or so.  The second thing that comes to mind is that there are a lot of distracting objects in this photo.  To list a few, the building on the left, the phone pole in the background, the driveway(?) on the right side and the yellow object that appears to be next to the driveway.  A lot of these things can be eliminated by using a wider aperture to throw the background out of focus.  I would increase the contrast and saturation also.  It might also help to just walk around the tree and take pictures from all different angles and distances and see what works the best.  It took me 4 days of trying to get pictures of my peach trees to get some shots that I liked.  What worked for me was a low angle and close to the tree.  Every tree is different and it will probably take time to find the best angle and lighting situation.  Usually early morning and late afternoon give the best light.  I hope some of this is helpful to you.  I gave this one a 4 but I think you could produce much better with a little more work. Greg', '2003-04-08 23:18:55.000000', NULL, 1),
