(264980, 17550, 7010, '<td valign="top">Good job kiwi! congrats!<br/>Er...oops, Michael I mean...<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2003-04-14 16:40:05.</i></td>', 'Good job kiwi! congrats!Er...oops, Michael I mean...', '2003-04-14 06:24:58.000000', '2003-04-14 17:40:05.000000', 0),
(264982, 17000, 2798, '<td valign="top">Thanks to everyone that voted on this shot including those of you that didn''t decode what I was trying to capture - it''s useful to me to know that my meaning wasn''t as clear as I''d hoped.<br/> <br/>For the record this picture is about racial equality which is why we have a patch of light and dark. It wasn''t intentional but I like how that line is blurred too. The coloured letters spell out blind which is supposed to suggest colour blindness (in a racial sense) and the fact that it''s in children''s letters is meant to suggest the basic simplicity of the equality ideal. Frisca spotted another twist to this shot that I''d not intended - the long shadows imply a dawning light as if the light is dawning on the idea.<br/> <br/>Finally the title is a refrain from Dr Martin Luther King Jr''s famous "I have a dream" speach which deals with racial equality.<br/> <br/>Thanks everyone for your comments and votes.</td>', 'Thanks to everyone that voted on this shot including those of you that didn''t decode what I was trying to capture - it''s useful to me to know that my meaning wasn''t as clear as I''d hoped. For the record this picture is about racial equality which is why we have a patch of light and dark. It wasn''t intentional but I like how that line is blurred too. The coloured letters spell out blind which is supposed to suggest colour blindness (in a racial sense) and the fact that it''s in children''s letters is meant to suggest the basic simplicity of the equality ideal. Frisca spotted another twist to this shot that I''d not intended - the long shadows imply a dawning light as if the light is dawning on the idea. Finally the title is a refrain from Dr Martin Luther King Jr''s famous "I have a dream" speach which deals with racial equality. Thanks everyone for your comments and votes.', '2003-04-14 06:26:16.000000', NULL, 0),
(264991, 17168, 3735, '<td valign="top">Thank you everyone for your vote, and most of all for your comments<br/>I am so happy with this score. Never in my wildest dream that i would place # 16 out of 295  WOW!!!!!!!  I wont sleep tonite  lol ---- Diana</td>', 'Thank you everyone for your vote, and most of all for your commentsI am so happy with this score. Never in my wildest dream that i would place # 16 out of 295  WOW!!!!!!!  I wont sleep tonite  lol ---- Diana', '2003-04-14 06:34:29.000000', NULL, 0),
(265022, 17314, 2798, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/> <br/>My very first impression of this is that I really can''t imagine a more fitting subject for the Colour challenge. So well done on that score - definitely meets the challenge in my view. There is perhaps some question about whether this is simply a photograph of someone else''s artwork but in my view this is both a collage of a number of people''s artwork plus it''s a real 3D structure as evidenced by the shadows and that makes this OK in my book. This seems to me to be a reflection of something about this neighborhood as well as being a celebration of how grafitti can actully be vibrant and attractive.<br/> <br/>The second thing that strikes me about your photo is the very strong importance of light and shadow. Somehow the shadows in the top left and bottom right frame the scene in a very interesting way that lifts the picture. Those shadows combine with the border to turn this shot into what is almost a painted canvas. The very strong sunlight has also really made those colours shine and that''s really what this picture is all about.<br/> <br/>Your focus seems good and exposure too - which might have been tricky considering the light. Your composition is very face-on but as I mention above it really works for this shot. It''s hard to know what your own intentions where with this picture or whether you succeeded with them but you''ve captured a wonderfully vibrant image with tons and tons of detail. Really something to stare at and keep finding new things.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club My very first impression of this is that I really can''t imagine a more fitting subject for the Colour challenge. So well done on that score - definitely meets the challenge in my view. There is perhaps some question about whether this is simply a photograph of someone else''s artwork but in my view this is both a collage of a number of people''s artwork plus it''s a real 3D structure as evidenced by the shadows and that makes this OK in my book. This seems to me to be a reflection of something about this neighborhood as well as being a celebration of how grafitti can actully be vibrant and attractive. The second thing that strikes me about your photo is the very strong importance of light and shadow. Somehow the shadows in the top left and bottom right frame the scene in a very interesting way that lifts the picture. Those shadows combine with the border to turn this shot into what is almost a painted canvas. The very strong sunlight has also really made those colours shine and that''s really what this picture is all about. Your focus seems good and exposure too - which might have been tricky considering the light. Your composition is very face-on but as I mention above it really works for this shot. It''s hard to know what your own intentions where with this picture or whether you succeeded with them but you''ve captured a wonderfully vibrant image with tons and tons of detail. Really something to stare at and keep finding new things.', '2003-04-14 07:29:34.000000', NULL, 0),
(265058, 17550, 4482, '<td valign="top">Congrats! I want a glass like that too!!  :-)</td>', 'Congrats! I want a glass like that too!!  :-)', '2003-04-14 07:51:37.000000', NULL, 0),
(265072, 17493, 5728, '<td valign="top">Wonderful photo. So which one is you then?</td>', 'Wonderful photo. So which one is you then?', '2003-04-14 08:02:57.000000', NULL, 0),
(265080, 17412, 5686, '<td valign="top">Background:<br/>In the fall 2002, I created a photo from several single photos from our<br/>flower garden.  It was printed at the photo lab, laminated and mounted on wood.  <br/> <br/>Current:<br/>With the colour challenge, I took the original soft copy of the created<br/>photo and converted the entire image to gray scale.  Drew in an oval of what to cut out and printed this new image on a black and white laser printer.<br/>Placed two sided tape on the back of the paper along the oval and cut the center out for a nice clean line.  Removed the backside of the tape and aligned the gray oval printout directly on top of the laminated mounted coloured photo.<br/> <br/>Now it was just a matter of taking the shot.  Tried several but this one was placed in direct sunlight and by closing the window blinds added the dark shading thru the photo.<br/> <br/>Hope that is clear enough, if not drop a line - gmacpro@yahoo.ca<br/></td>', 'Background:In the fall 2002, I created a photo from several single photos from ourflower garden.  It was printed at the photo lab, laminated and mounted on wood.   Current:With the colour challenge, I took the original soft copy of the createdphoto and converted the entire image to gray scale.  Drew in an oval of what to cut out and printed this new image on a black and white laser printer.Placed two sided tape on the back of the paper along the oval and cut the center out for a nice clean line.  Removed the backside of the tape and aligned the gray oval printout directly on top of the laminated mounted coloured photo. Now it was just a matter of taking the shot.  Tried several but this one was placed in direct sunlight and by closing the window blinds added the dark shading thru the photo. Hope that is clear enough, if not drop a line - gmacpro@yahoo.ca', '2003-04-14 08:06:17.000000', NULL, 0),
(265115, 17291, 7543, '<td valign="top">This was my first post, and I didn''t do much in photoshop this time. Should probably have spent some time on adjusting levels. This setup of the helmets wasn´t done by me, I took the shot at a gallery in iceland, where the helmets were a part of a bigger piece. Thanks for all the comments, they are a great help to me. <br/></td>', 'This was my first post, and I didn''t do much in photoshop this time. Should probably have spent some time on adjusting levels. This setup of the helmets wasn´t done by me, I took the shot at a gallery in iceland, where the helmets were a part of a bigger piece. Thanks for all the comments, they are a great help to me. ', '2003-04-14 08:23:25.000000', NULL, 0),
(265149, 17305, 1829, '<td valign="top">Andrew, Congrats on your well earned ribbon.  Excellent shot.  Nice captrure.</td>', 'Andrew, Congrats on your well earned ribbon.  Excellent shot.  Nice captrure.', '2003-04-14 08:49:06.000000', NULL, 0),
(265182, 17462, 5728, '<td valign="top">Critique Club guten Tag:<br/> <br/>Hiya Stephan, well I get to critique your photo. First of all congratulations on an excellent calla shot, (I only found out for the first time last week from John that these flowers are called callas).<br/> <br/>The composition of this pic is very good. I love the way the flower curves in from the right side and flows into the center of the image. The negative space on the left is justified here and it gives the flower breathing room and I think anything less would have given this photo a cramped feeling.<br/> <br/>The choice of blue for the background was ideal. Yellow and blue always go well together, you can never go wrong using that combination. The border is not overdone at all and fits in well especially with the added effect of the border disolving into the negative space from the left hand side.<br/> <br/>There is a jagged edge on the top petal on the left which is a little disturbing if you look at it long enough, and the background blue is a tad noisy. But these are only "Kleinigkeiten" which don''t really lower the quality of this image at all, just that I had to find something to "mecker" about right :-)<br/> <br/>Overall and excellent image, one that is a pleasure to look at, and one you should definitely start offering as a print! It has also just jumped on to my favourite photo list.<br/> <br/>Schöne Grüsse<br/> <br/>Gary<br/> <br/></td>', 'Critique Club guten Tag: Hiya Stephan, well I get to critique your photo. First of all congratulations on an excellent calla shot, (I only found out for the first time last week from John that these flowers are called callas). The composition of this pic is very good. I love the way the flower curves in from the right side and flows into the center of the image. The negative space on the left is justified here and it gives the flower breathing room and I think anything less would have given this photo a cramped feeling. The choice of blue for the background was ideal. Yellow and blue always go well together, you can never go wrong using that combination. The border is not overdone at all and fits in well especially with the added effect of the border disolving into the negative space from the left hand side. There is a jagged edge on the top petal on the left which is a little disturbing if you look at it long enough, and the background blue is a tad noisy. But these are only "Kleinigkeiten" which don''t really lower the quality of this image at all, just that I had to find something to "mecker" about right :-) Overall and excellent image, one that is a pleasure to look at, and one you should definitely start offering as a print! It has also just jumped on to my favourite photo list. Schöne Grüsse Gary ', '2003-04-14 09:20:46.000000', NULL, 0),
(265193, 17040, 2716, '<td valign="top">Wow!  Thanks for all the nice comments.</td>', 'Wow!  Thanks for all the nice comments.', '2003-04-14 09:35:09.000000', NULL, 0),
