(265305, 17269, 7536, '<td valign="top">Thanks for the great feedback. I used the good ole'' Testors paints to set this one up. Yes, I do paint models in my spare time, thought not as much as I used to (as evidenced by the light blue). The color choices were completely random, although I did do some re-arranging to balance things out a bit. I tried not to put similar colors next to each other. I also tried a dark background, but settled on the lighter one because it brought out the contours of the bottles a little better. The "Made in USA" labels were turned to the front on purpose to make a veiled political statement in support of the troops.</td>', 'Thanks for the great feedback. I used the good ole'' Testors paints to set this one up. Yes, I do paint models in my spare time, thought not as much as I used to (as evidenced by the light blue). The color choices were completely random, although I did do some re-arranging to balance things out a bit. I tried not to put similar colors next to each other. I also tried a dark background, but settled on the lighter one because it brought out the contours of the bottles a little better. The "Made in USA" labels were turned to the front on purpose to make a veiled political statement in support of the troops.', '2003-04-14 11:26:31.000000', NULL, 0),
(265398, 17493, 7523, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by kiwiness:</b></div><hr/><i>Wonderful photo. So which one is you then?</i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>I''m the one with the dark hair.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2003-04-14 11:35:39.</i></td>', 'Originally posted by kiwiness:Wonderful photo. So which one is you then?  I''m the one with the dark hair.', '2003-04-14 11:53:10.000000', '2003-04-14 12:35:39.000000', 0),
(265429, 17580, 4852, '<td valign="top">Nice job, this in no way deserves a single 1 or any 2''s. Those brought your score down. You should have placed higher in my opinion.</td>', 'Nice job, this in no way deserves a single 1 or any 2''s. Those brought your score down. You should have placed higher in my opinion.', '2003-04-14 12:33:55.000000', NULL, 0),
(265444, 17380, 1280, '<td valign="top">Critique Club comments by Grayce<br/>Wow!  This is a very interesting capture you''ve got here.  It reminds me of an ad for the cruise line.  <br/>The stark contrast between the grey/blue, cold, cloudy portion of sky, and the warm, orange, vibrant part is amazing. And the way the rays seem to envelope the ship is something you just don''t see all the time.<br/>The water looks very choppy, and may be a bit over-sharpened? The metallic coloring of it, though, adds more interest to the image.<br/>The composition would benefit from the ship not being so centered. I think I''d crop part of the water on the bottom, to lower the ship to the bottom third.<br/>OVer all I like it.  Good eye!<br/>Regards,<br/> <br/>Grayce</td>', 'Critique Club comments by GrayceWow!  This is a very interesting capture you''ve got here.  It reminds me of an ad for the cruise line.  The stark contrast between the grey/blue, cold, cloudy portion of sky, and the warm, orange, vibrant part is amazing. And the way the rays seem to envelope the ship is something you just don''t see all the time.The water looks very choppy, and may be a bit over-sharpened? The metallic coloring of it, though, adds more interest to the image.The composition would benefit from the ship not being so centered. I think I''d crop part of the water on the bottom, to lower the ship to the bottom third.OVer all I like it.  Good eye!Regards, Grayce', '2003-04-14 12:43:25.000000', NULL, 0),
(265452, 17293, 6519, '<td valign="top">To everyone who thinks the pic is out of focus: Have another look at it! The little bubble in the center is in perfect focus. <br/>The rest of it really looked that way, it''s in the nature of the experiment - it constantly changes (and looks totally different every time you start a new one) as the chemicals reactions are happening, and at that point it had this "soft focus" look to it. <br/>But I understand that abstracts like this, especially when you don''t know what it actually IS, are not everyone''s cup of tea. <br/>Makes a cute desktop background, anyway :-)</td>', 'To everyone who thinks the pic is out of focus: Have another look at it! The little bubble in the center is in perfect focus. The rest of it really looked that way, it''s in the nature of the experiment - it constantly changes (and looks totally different every time you start a new one) as the chemicals reactions are happening, and at that point it had this "soft focus" look to it. But I understand that abstracts like this, especially when you don''t know what it actually IS, are not everyone''s cup of tea. Makes a cute desktop background, anyway :-)', '2003-04-14 12:46:14.000000', NULL, 0),
(265455, 17580, 5865, '<td valign="top">I agree with BadPigg.  I can''t understand why anyone would score you 1 or 2 for this shot.</td>', 'I agree with BadPigg.  I can''t understand why anyone would score you 1 or 2 for this shot.', '2003-04-14 12:47:16.000000', NULL, 0),
(265473, 17072, 3540, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/>By Inspzil<br/> <br/>Composition - One of my fav''s of the crayon pics this week.  I really think the framing was the key in this shot.  It shows enough of the crayon but not too much.  I think maybe having the crayons go all the way across the frame would''ve been a good alternate to having only a few on each side of the blue one.  I think if you would''ve done something like that though, the aperture would have to be turned down to get the "select" focus on just the blue one.  I don''t know if that''s a real photography term, but I think you know what I mean.  But as it is it is a solid composition.<br/> <br/>Technical - Well taken.  This is one where most folks would choose the shallow or great DOF.  You chose in the middle.  I think any way you did it it would work ok.  With more crayons, I''d say go shallower, and with this many, if I did this pic, I''d have made it greater.  I think its all a matter of personal preference though.  Good work in the editing of this photo to do the desat of all colors but blue.  Great idea.<br/> <br/>Overall - I really liked this photo and think you got the short end of the crayon in your score.  It is not a candidate for "winner" like your pi pic, but definitely a very good photo.  The only thing I find odd about this as I look at it one last time is the white crayon has a little different hue to it than the others.  Could be just the shadows but I suspect it might have just an iota of blue in it to keep it from completely desaturating.  All in all I think its a good pic.  Keep up the good work. - Inspzil</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique ClubBy Inspzil Composition - One of my fav''s of the crayon pics this week.  I really think the framing was the key in this shot.  It shows enough of the crayon but not too much.  I think maybe having the crayons go all the way across the frame would''ve been a good alternate to having only a few on each side of the blue one.  I think if you would''ve done something like that though, the aperture would have to be turned down to get the "select" focus on just the blue one.  I don''t know if that''s a real photography term, but I think you know what I mean.  But as it is it is a solid composition. Technical - Well taken.  This is one where most folks would choose the shallow or great DOF.  You chose in the middle.  I think any way you did it it would work ok.  With more crayons, I''d say go shallower, and with this many, if I did this pic, I''d have made it greater.  I think its all a matter of personal preference though.  Good work in the editing of this photo to do the desat of all colors but blue.  Great idea. Overall - I really liked this photo and think you got the short end of the crayon in your score.  It is not a candidate for "winner" like your pi pic, but definitely a very good photo.  The only thing I find odd about this as I look at it one last time is the white crayon has a little different hue to it than the others.  Could be just the shadows but I suspect it might have just an iota of blue in it to keep it from completely desaturating.  All in all I think its a good pic.  Keep up the good work. - Inspzil', '2003-04-14 12:55:35.000000', NULL, 0),
(265474, 17580, 772, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club :)<br/> <br/>Hi Geocide...<br/> <br/>This is an excellent color shot... I really like the abstract nature of it.  It took me a while to figure out what I was looking at translucent and colored CD cases...  <br/> <br/>I read through all your ''nice job'' comments :)  No one really wanted to step up to the plate for you on the comments this week...  <br/> <br/>Compositionally, I think you could have created a higher impact by going with a square frame rather than rectangluar.  Why?  Because the ''centered'' composition can be a little awkward sometimes.  I believe that when you have an image such as this one, using a square frame with less empty space on both sides works better.  Your image is still centered, but it doesn''t really jump up and scream ''centered orientation'' when it''s in a tighter square area.  <br/> <br/>Keep up the good work :)<br/> <br/>John Setzler<br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club :) Hi Geocide... This is an excellent color shot... I really like the abstract nature of it.  It took me a while to figure out what I was looking at translucent and colored CD cases...   I read through all your ''nice job'' comments :)  No one really wanted to step up to the plate for you on the comments this week...   Compositionally, I think you could have created a higher impact by going with a square frame rather than rectangluar.  Why?  Because the ''centered'' composition can be a little awkward sometimes.  I believe that when you have an image such as this one, using a square frame with less empty space on both sides works better.  Your image is still centered, but it doesn''t really jump up and scream ''centered orientation'' when it''s in a tighter square area.   Keep up the good work :) John Setzler', '2003-04-14 12:56:14.000000', NULL, 0),
(265507, 17291, 7543, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by K-Rob:</b></div><hr/><i>I think red, blue, red, blue (or vice versa) would''ve given a better effect but I like it a lot none the less.</i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>This wasn''t my setup, and I didn''t want to change anything, take the observer point of view if you will....<br/></td>', 'Originally posted by K-Rob:I think red, blue, red, blue (or vice versa) would''ve given a better effect but I like it a lot none the less.  This wasn''t my setup, and I didn''t want to change anything, take the observer point of view if you will....', '2003-04-14 13:07:00.000000', NULL, 0),
(265526, 17405, 1122, '<td valign="top">Thanks all for the comment.  When I review this shot after reading all the comments, I agree that the reflection on water is  a bit distracting.  At the first place, I thought the reflection could add some mood to the photo.  But may be that''s too much.  I should use a polarizer to tune down the reflection a bit.  But I still wanna retain some of the reflection coz that''s one of the reason I choose this photo.  Thanks again for your votesss~  See you all in next chanllenge~ :D</td>', 'Thanks all for the comment.  When I review this shot after reading all the comments, I agree that the reflection on water is  a bit distracting.  At the first place, I thought the reflection could add some mood to the photo.  But may be that''s too much.  I should use a polarizer to tune down the reflection a bit.  But I still wanna retain some of the reflection coz that''s one of the reason I choose this photo.  Thanks again for your votesss~  See you all in next chanllenge~ :D', '2003-04-14 13:13:37.000000', NULL, 0),
(265598, 17352, 6064, '<td valign="top">I would love to blur the backgrounds but unfortuantely this is nearly impossible with the A40<br/> <br/>I thought that the brick gave a nice texture to the picture. :) Oh well :)</td>', 'I would love to blur the backgrounds but unfortuantely this is nearly impossible with the A40 I thought that the brick gave a nice texture to the picture. :) Oh well :)', '2003-04-14 13:59:38.000000', NULL, 0),
